INT. FOG-SIGNAL STATION - LIGHTHOUSE BASE - NIGHT

A round stone room the size of a chapel, its walls sweating. A brass foghorn compressor squats in the center like a sleeping animal. Oil lamps gutter on iron hooks. Everything smells of kelp and rust.

ORVILLE HASKELL (60s, gaunt, a keeper's wool coat gone green at the seams) kneels before the compressor with a wrench, his breath smoking.

MRS. DELPHINE VOSS (50s, widow's black, pearl buttons, a face carved from candle wax) stands in the doorway holding a covered birdcage.

                    HASKELL
          You shouldn't have rowed out in this.

                    DELPHINE
          Neither should you have stayed.

She sets the cage on a crate. Something inside shifts and settles.

                    HASKELL
          It stopped knocking an hour ago.

                    DELPHINE
          (quietly)
          Then it has what it wanted.

A shortwave radio on the shelf crackles. Haskell flinches, then crosses to it.

                    TAMSIN (V.O.)
          (through static)
          Station Nine, this is Coast Guard Relay. Do you read? Over.

Haskell thumbs the key.

                    HASKELL
          Relay, Station Nine. Fog is lifting. All quiet here.

                    TAMSIN (V.O.)
          Copy, quiet. Your lamp is out, Mr. Haskell. We show no light from the tower.

Haskell looks up the spiral stair. Black. Delphine lifts the cloth from the birdcage. Empty. The little door hangs open.

                    DELPHINE
          Tell her we'll light it at dawn.

                    HASKELL
          (into radio)
          At dawn, Relay. Station Nine out.

He clicks the radio off. Far above them, slow and deliberate, something descends one step at a time.

                    DELPHINE
          (barely audible)
          Gentle now. He is only tired.

Haskell sits on the crate, the wrench loose in his hand, and watches the stair with the patience of a man who has run out of fear.
